    "case_id": "59",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old female presented with a 15-day history of a painful mass in the left breast, accompanied by a sinus tract and low-grade fever. She had been treated with empiric antibiotic therapy during this period, but her symptoms persisted. There was no family history of breast cancer. Physical examination showed a tender, erythematous mass in the left breast with an associated sinus tract. No palpable lymphadenopathy was noted in the left axillary region. Ultrasound of the left breast revealed an ill-defined, hypoechoic, heterogeneous 3 cm lesion in the upper-inner quadrant, a 5 mm sinus tract connecting the lesion to the skin, and a 4 mm focal ductal dilatation in the upper-outer quadrant. Mammography indicated generalized increased radioopacity throughout the left breast. Fine needle aspiration (FNA) showed inflammatory cells, including neutrophils, lymphocytes, and abundant macrophages. The left breast mass was subsequently excised through an open surgical biopsy.\n\nThe histopathologic evaluation of the specimen revealed granulomas with central caseaous necrosis, epitheloid histiocytes, Langhans' giant cells, and an intense lymphocytic infiltration at the periphery of the granulomas.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
